findings.message:
In parse_playlist(), when handling HLS `-hls_flags append_list` for subtitle (.vtt) playlists, the code derives `vtt_basename` from `vs->vtt_basename` (the user-configured VTT filename template, e.g. via `-hls_segment_filename`/`vtt_basename` option or its default derived from output naming) and passes it directly as the *format string* argument to `snprintf()`:

    snprintf(vtt_file, len, vtt_basename, vtt_index);

Because `vtt_basename` is attacker- or user-influenced content (it can contain arbitrary characters including `%` format specifiers, e.g. if the playlist/segment naming pattern is derived from untrusted input or contains unexpected `%` sequences), this is a classic uncontrolled format string vulnerability (CWE-134). If `vtt_basename` contains conversion specifiers that do not match the single `int vtt_index` argument supplied (e.g. `%s`, `%n`, or multiple `%d`), this can lead to out-of-bounds reads, a crash, information disclosure, or in the worst case (with `%n`) memory corruption. Even in the intended case where `vtt_basename` contains exactly one `%d`-style specifier intended as the segment/index placeholder, using it as the raw format string rather than as a filename literal with a separate fixed format string is unsafe because any other `%` characters incidentally present in the basename (e.g. filenames containing `%` from URLs, timestamps, or user-provided output templates) are also interpreted as format directives.

